deep.reconstruction:
This page continues the 5 June 1986 attempt to axiomatize a 'model of dimension 1' of a topological 'form'. It is the first step of a recursion on n, in which a 0-dimensional form is a discrete set of 'lieux' (places).

The data of the 1-dimensional model are:
(1) a set L of places;
(2) a subset L_0 of critical or branching places;
(3) a symmetric relation 'x and y are disjoint';
(4) a finer symmetric relation 'x and y are adjacent';
(5) a specialization relation 'x is a specialization of y', which forces x critical and y non-critical;
(6) a set S of 'segments';
(7) for each segment I, a 2-element set δI ⊂ L of mutually adjacent places, its extremities;
(8) a subset Ĩ ⊂ L, the places 'on the segment', with δI ⊂ Ĩ.

The page then gives three remarks (NB):
- NB1: x and y are adjacent iff there is I ∈ S with δI = {x,y}. Datum (4) is therefore redundant. Axiom (7) should then require that the two extremities be disjoint.
- NB2: Grothendieck presumes that, for adjacent x and y, at most two segments have δI = {x,y}. Exactly two occur iff x and y lie on a connected component of the form X that is (a model of) a circle.
- NB3: He is unsure whether I is determined by (δI, Ĩ), i.e. whether S may be regarded as a subset of P_2(L) × P(L).

Under 'Axiomes (en vrac)', E1 states that, on L, the relation 'x and y are not disjoint' is an equivalence relation. Its quotient is apparently the set of points of the form. Several distinct critical places may define the same critical point.

So places are finer than points, rather like branches or places over a singular point of a curve. Points are recovered as equivalence classes of overlapping places. The structure is a combinatorial, finite-topological-space-like encoding of a 1-dimensional stratified ('tame') space, i.e. a graph with branch points. The next page restarts the definition more economically with L, S, the maps I ↦ Ĩ and ∂: S → P_2(L), and the regular/singular decomposition.
